51644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,2629,315,279,9904,311,198,78045,17,1124,15940,61,18,856,481,220,18,1124,15940,856,284,481,59,37018,90,18,15170,17,92,1124,15940,220,17,87,59,60,258,279,9873,400,15,1124,273,856,1124,273,220,17,1124,2493,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of the solutions to\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\]in the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2 \\pi.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d50fb52595b342df7bd590fe9bef5cf82357be1e6cb2f0131665e44e20b85987"},"seconds":50.22169470787048,"seed":1075666808021344433,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \\(2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2x\\) in the interval \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\pi\\), we will approach the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, recall the double angle identity for sine: \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\). Substituting this into the equation, we get:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[2 \\sin^3 x - 3 \\sin x = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nWe can factor out \\(\\sin x\\) from both sides:\n\\[\\sin x (2 \\sin^2 x - 3) = -3 \\sin x \\cos x.\\]\nIf \\(\\sin x = 0\\), then \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\) are solutions. We will check these later. Assuming \\(\\sin x \\neq 0\\), we can divide both sides by \\(\\sin x\\):\n\\[2 \\sin^2 x - 3 = -3 \\cos x.\\]\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[2 \\sin^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 3 = 0.\\]\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos^2 x\\), we substitute:\n\\[2 (1 - \\cos^2 x) + 3 \\cos x - 3 = 0.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[2 - 2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 3 = 0,\\]\nor\n\\[-2 \\cos^2 x + 3 \\cos x - 1 = 0.\\]\nMultiplying through by -1, we get:\n\\[2 \\cos^2 x - 3 \\cos x + 1 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(\\cos x\\). We can solve it using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\cos x\\):\n\\[2y^2 - 3y + 1 = 0.\\]\nThe quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\) gives:\n\\[y = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 8}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm 1}{4}.\\]\nSo, the solutions are:\n\\[y = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\nThus, \\(\\cos x = 1\\) or \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nFor \\(\\cos x = 1\\), we have \\(x = 0, 2\\pi\\).\nFor \\(\\cos x = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we have \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nWe also need to check the solutions where \\(\\sin x = 0\\), which are \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi\\). Combining all solutions, we have \\(x = 0, \\pi, 2\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nThe sum of these solutions is:\n\\[0 + \\pi + 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + 2\\pi = 4\\pi.\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{4\\pi}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4\\pi}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
